zfs_rebase rebases one ZFS filesystem onto another. Given two sides
that diverged from a common base, it replays the changes of one side
onto the other, or reports the names it could not decide and why.

The base is not given: it is the branch point, and the tool derives it
from the two sides' origin chains. The decision is made over names,
over hardlink pools and over content, and its one output is a
manifest of actions and conflicts. That manifest is applied either to
a read-only clone of the onto snapshot, leaving the user's datasets
untouched, or to the onto dataset itself with a pre-apply snapshot
kept as the before-image.

A rebase is durable rather than a process's leavings: the result
carries a record in user properties, each input snapshot is held for
the rebase's whole life, and the run's progress is a sequence of
gates a kill leaves behind, so that it can be continued, restarted,
verified or aborted afterwards. The tool never promotes; adopting the
result is the user's own work.

Building it needs the FreeBSD source tree, not just the installed
headers: the port compiles against sys/contrib/openzfs under SRC_BASE
(/usr/src by default) the way FreeBSD's own zfs(8) is built. The tool
links against base's libzfs, libzfs_core and libnvpair, execs
nothing, and must run as root.
